-- title:
--   Linear disjointness of F and an algebraic K' over K
-- statement:
--   Let $K$, $F$, $K'$, $F'$ be fields with $F$ an algebra over $K$, $F'$ an algebra over $K'$, $K'$ an algebra over $K$, and $F'$ an algebra over both $F$ and $K$, the scalar actions being compatible in the sense that $F'$ is a scalar tower over $K \subseteq K'$ and over $K \subseteq F$. Assume $K$ is perfect and $K'$ is algebraic over $K$. Assume further the hypothesis [`AlgebraicCurve.ConstantsAreBase K F`](def/AlgebraicCurve_AdelicIndex.html#L45), i.e. that the Riemann–Roch space of the zero divisor in $\mathrm{Place}\,K\,F \to_{0} \mathbb{Z}$ — the $K$-submodule $L(0)$ of $F$ — coincides with the range of the $K$-linear map $K \to F$, so that the constants of $F/K$ are exactly $K$. Then for every index type $\iota$ and every family $b : \iota \to F$ which is linearly independent over $K$, the family $i \mapsto \mathrm{algebraMap}\,F\,F'\,(b\,i)$ of its images in $F'$ is linearly independent over $K'$. No hypothesis is made that $F'$ is generated by $F$ and $K'$, nor that $F$ or $F'$ is a function field.
--
--   This is the statement that $F$ and $K'$ are linearly disjoint over $K$ inside $F'$, under the hypotheses that $K$ is perfect and algebraically closed in $F$ in the strong form $L(0) = K$, and that $K'/K$ is algebraic. It is used in the study of places under a constant field extension, in particular by [`AlgebraicCurve.Place.exists_comap_algebraMap_eq_of_constantFieldExtension_of_deg_eq_one_of_isAlgebraic`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.Place.exists_comap_algebraMap_eq_of_constantFieldExtension_of_deg_eq_one_of_isAlgebraic).
